# Editable physical notebook page profiles

## Scope and existing evidence

Connect configurable sheet size, paper pattern, spacing, top/bottom/left/right
margins, border rounding, dates, page numbers, and printed-sheet flags to Rust
profile values.

The historical theoretical design remains separate from the executable
implementation. This record was closed after native Rust geometry and CLI
accepted/refused cases passed; it does not prove full notebook rendering.

## Acceptance decision

Completed — practical native Rust CLI acceptance on November 3, 2026.

The canonical Rust paper profile owns sheet dimensions, grid/ruled pattern,
spacing and independent top, bottom, left and right margins. The composite
notebook profile adds left/right date line/box placement, page numbers,
printed-sheet handling and rounded printable borders; exported PDF/PNG paper
layers share the native Rust geometry. The existing localhost editor can
edit the paper and notebook settings without a second rendering authority.

Accepted example: the focused native CLI test saved and reopened grid and
ruled notebooks with 5 mm and 7 mm spacing, differing four-edge margins,
top-left line and top-right box dates, 3 mm rounded borders, page numbering
and two-page PDF output. Each PDF exactly matched the same Rust paper-layer
renderer; grid and ruled PDF bytes differed.

Refused example: a paper profile with 204 mm left plus 14 mm right margins on
a 210 mm sheet was rejected before an explicitly replaceable output sentinel
was touched. Existing date-clearance, border and printed-sheet tests also
remain in the CLI/notebook-profile harness.

Verification: `cargo test --offline -p atrament_profile_editor_cli --bin
atrament-profiles` passed 25 tests and strict all-test Clippy passed. This
accepts only bounded physical page geometry, not handwriting stroke samples,
actual date text, image overlays, printer drivers, or cross-platform support.
Historical design evidence remains not independently re-audited.
